WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.340
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:07.569
Today we're going to talk about explosions.

00:00:07.569 --> 00:00:12.639
To be more precise, imagine that we already
have the physics simulation data for an explosion

00:00:12.639 --> 00:00:16.180
on our computer, but we would like to visualize
it on our screen.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:20.970
This requires a light simulation program that
is able to create an image of this virtual

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:25.010
scene that looks exactly the same as it would
in reality.

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:29.890
We have had plenty of earlier episodes on
light transport, and as you know all too well,

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:31.759
it is one of my favorite topics.

00:00:31.759 --> 00:00:33.540
I just can't get enough of it.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:37.090
I've put a link to these related episodes
in the video description.

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:41.890
If we wish to render a huge smoke plume, we
perform something that computer graphics people

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:44.550
call volumetric light transport.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:49.580
This means that a ray of light doesn't necessarily
bounce off of the surface of materials, but

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:53.670
it can penetrate their surfaces and scatter
around inside of them.

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:58.460
A technique that can deal with this is called
volumetric path tracing, and if we wish to

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:03.860
create an image of an explosion using that,
well, better pack some fast food because it

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:06.480
is likely going to take several hours.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:11.450
The explosion in this image took 13 hours
and it is still not rendered perfectly.

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:18.230
But this technique is able to solve this problem
in 20 minutes, which is almost 40 times quicker.

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:19.300
Unbelievable.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:24.840
The key idea is that this super complicated
volumetric explosion data can be reimagined

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.440
as a large batch of point light sources.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:31.880
If we solve this light transport problem between
these point light sources, we get a solution

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:37.180
that is remarkably similar to the original
solution with path tracing, however, solving

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:40.060
this new representation is much simpler.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:41.750
But that's only the first step.

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:45.970
If we have a bunch of light sources, we can
create a grid structure around them, and in

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:51.040
these gridpoints, we can compute shadows and
illumination in a highly efficient manner.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.540
What's more, we can create multiple of these
grid representations.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:02:00.700
They all work on the very same data, but some
of them are finer, and some of them are significantly

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:02.710
sparser, more coarse.

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:07.470
Another smart observation here is that even
though sharp, high-frequency illumination

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:13.020
details need to be computed on this fine grid,
which takes quite a bit of computation time,

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:18.580
it is sufficient to solve the coarse, low-frequency
details on one of these sparser grids.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:23.770
The results look indistinguishable from the
ground truth solutions, but the overall computation

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:26.090
time is significantly reduced.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:30.100
The paper contains detailed comparisons against
other techniques as well.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:34.720
Most of these scenes are rendered using hundreds
of thousands of these point light sources,

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.910
and as you can see, the results are unbelievable.

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:42.100
If you would like to learn even more about
light transport, I am holding a Master-level

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:46.070
course on this at the Vienna University of
Technology in Austria.

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:50.870
I thought that the teachings should not only
be available for those 30 people who sit in

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.900
the room, who can afford a university education.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.400
It should be available for everyone.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:00.990
So, we made the entirety of the lecture available
for everyone, free of charge, and I am so

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:06.110
glad to see that thousands of people have
watched it, and to this day I get many messages

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:09.819
that they enjoyed it and now they see the
world differently.

00:03:09.819 --> 00:03:14.319
It was recorded live with the students in
the room, and it doesn't have the audio quality

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:15.760
of Two Minute Papers.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:20.520
However, what it does well, is it conjures
up the atmosphere of these lectures and you

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.430
can almost feel like one of the students sitting
there.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:27.330
If you're interested, have a look, the link
is available in the video description!

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:30.200
And make sure to read this paper too, it's
incredible.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:34.320
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

